## Phase 3 — Subagents: additional contexts running in parallel

Pass-through plus validation. `SubAgent` upstream takes `name`, `description`, `systemPrompt`, `tools`, `model`, `middleware`, `interruptOn`, `skills`, `responseFormat`.

### Guidelines

- **Validate what upstream won't.** Duplicate names, empty `description`, unresolvable model refs → `AgentConfigError` at construction.
- **The `description` field is the number-one failure mode.** It is what the parent model sees when choosing. Vague description → the parent never delegates, or delegates wrong. Document loudly in JSDoc; it is a docs problem you own.
- **Know the skills inheritance rule and document it:** custom subagents do **not** inherit the parent's skills. Only the general-purpose subagent does. This surprises people.
- **Async subagents stay unexposed initially.** `graphId`-based specs are auto-separated at runtime and imply a deployed graph. Add when someone asks.
- **Name the parent agent.** Pass `name` through to `createDeepAgent` so `createSubagentTransformer` can surface nested runs on `run.subagents` when streaming — observability is the main thing consumers lose by delegating.
- **Document the trade honestly:** delegation costs latency (serial round-trips) and debuggability (the parent loses sight of child reasoning). The win is context isolation plus model-tier routing — grunt work on a cheap model, orchestration on the expensive one.

### Acceptance
- Duplicate names, empty descriptions rejected at construction
- Per-subagent `model` and `tools` resolve through your `ModelSpec`/`ToolSpec` path
- Nested runs appear on `run.subagents` when streaming

